        "\n",
        "# 5 Analysis\n",
        "\n",
        "Throughout the analysis, we derive numerical features from 1-grams of the cleaned review texts, i.e. variables taken from individual words. This keeps the feature engineering simple enough, avoids high dimensionality in the feature space and reduces computation times. More in-depth approaches not considered here may also include 2-grams or 3-grams, and thus variables derived from chains of multiple words.\n",
        "\n",
        "## 5.1 Train-test split\n",
        "\n",
        "The data is split into a training set and a test set before any further preprocessing of the cleaned texts. To ensure that the test set contains at least 1,000 observations, we consider a **test share** of **11%**, resulting in a **test sample size** of **1,048**. For simplicity, we only consider such a fixed split; more elaborate but also time- and memory-consuming approaches use nested cross-validation ideas with multiple training-test splits. For the purpose of this project, the size of the random test sample is, however, large enough to allow for a generalization of the findings. The split here is stratified, ensuring that both the training and the test sample reflect the 11% and 89% shares of `Good` and `Bad` reviews in the total dataset.\n",
        "\n",
        "<details><summary>Show Python code</summary>"
      ],
